Yajman:

  has language charter: # yajman.yjml v3.4 — meta-spec for YJML; examples: es_modules.yjml, todos_app.yjml

    has system scope rule:
      - should treat Yajman as a language for drafting any system in the world in one tree
      - should not limit Yajman to software; apps, drivers, engines, buildings, tools, and processes are all valid subjects
      - should name the chosen system once at the root of each <system>.yjml document

    has totality of description rule:
      - should use the tree to describe the system as a whole (parts, layout, roles, relationships)
      - should put most descriptive prose in # comments on structural ('has') lines
      - should use structural and behavioral nodes for intangible systems when the entity itself carries the description

    has totality of specification rule:
      - should use the tree to state every required behaviour of the system
      - should rely on the split between structural nodes and behavioral nodes to hold the full specification
      - should write labels and '- should' text in language specific to the system under documentation
      - should write this meta document in domain-neutral language and defer system-specific terms to product trees
      - should prefer a deeper tree of small assemblies over a flat list of many siblings at one level
      - should put optional code paths and symbols in # comments when the system is a program

  has language definition: # syntax, structure, and behaviour rules

    has dual node syntax: # only two node kinds exist in YJML

      has structural node form: # noun node
        - should start a structural line with the keyword 'has', then an owner label, then a colon
        - should add an optional # comment after the colon when more description is needed
        - should nest child structural nodes or behavioral lines beneath that owner

      has behavioral node form: # verb node
        - should start a behavioral line with a hyphen, then the keyword 'should', then the requirement text
        - should indent each behavioral line under the structural parent that owns that requirement
        - should not attach behavioral lines without a parent structural owner

      has document envelope rule: # V1 V2 V14 — file shape
        - should keep exactly one top-level key (the system name) and nest the whole tree under it
        - should not express requirements using YAML shapes other than 'has' lines and '- should' lines
        - should indent two spaces per level
        - should allow '&anchor' on a 'has' line and '*anchor' in a '- should' line when one part refers to another
        - should note that unquoted colons inside '- should' text may break strict YAML parsers; quote those lines or use a YJML-aware linter

    has structural node language: # what a 'has' owner may name

      has entity naming rules:

        has physical entity rule: # V3
          - should name each structural owner after a physical part, place, assembly, mechanism, or role in the real system
          - should not use 'has' labels for phases, journeys, or vague areas with no referent in the system

        has owner label rule: # V4
          - should write owner labels in normal case (words with spaces), like fuel pump or project root validator
          - should not use camelCase or PascalCase in an owner label
          - should not use a filesystem path or a full English sentence as the label
          - should prefer role or part names over implementation artifact names in the label when both are possible
          - should use the # on a structural line for extra description and optional traceability (drawing, code symbol, etc.)

      has assembly rules:

        has composite entity rule: # V4a
          - should add a parent structural owner for the whole assembly and child structural owners for distinct parts or sub-roles
          - should give each child the '- should' lines that belong to that part, not to a vague parent
          - should usually leave assembly parents with child structural nodes only, not a long mixed '- should' list
          - should put '&anchor' on a structural owner that other owners reference with '*anchor'
          - should not split the same product into parallel branches for capability versus implementation (wrong: one branch for business job and another for cli implementation)
          - should nest command intake, validation, planning, and execution under one assembly when they are one end-to-end behaviour

        has composition ordering rule: # V5 V16
          - should order direct sibling 'has' children by time flow, assembly order, or larger-to-smaller scope (pick one rule per parent and stay consistent)
          - should list core or enclosing parts before peripheral or smaller parts
          - should nest from the assembly down to the part before naming a small control on that part
          - should not hang a detail on the system root when it belongs inside an intermediate owner

      has nesting rules: # V10 V12 — depth over width

        has nesting depth rule:
          - should target two or three direct child 'has' owners per parent on the first structuring pass
          - should add intermediate parents and go deeper instead of listing four or more siblings at one level
          - should allow a fourth direct child 'has' only when three children cannot express the relationship and the reader can see why
          - should treat four children on one parent as rare; a whole document should have at most one such parent, or none
          - should allow a parent with zero child 'has' owners when it only has '- should' lines
          - should nest from the whole system down to the smallest owned part that still carries requirements

        has nesting refinement rule:
          - should review parents with three children before adding a fourth
          - should prefer a new intermediate 'has' owner (with two or three children) over a fourth sibling
          - should re-run this review until the tree is deep, mostly two-or-three-wide, and four-wide only where unavoidable

        has lineage purpose rule:
          - should treat a structural owner's purpose as the plain story told by reading its whole subtree down to every '- should' leaf
          - should not rely on the parent # comment to explain what the parent is for; the lineage must carry that meaning
          - should delete, merge, or relocate a parent when the deduced purpose is only that it groups children and adds no story the lineage needs
          - should rename a parent when the deduced purpose from lineage does not match the owner label
          - should merge sibling branches when lineage shows the same end-to-end job split only by layer names (surface, writer, implementation)
          - should run this lineage-purpose check on every refactor iteration before keeping an intermediate assembly

    has behavioral node language: # what a '- should' line must do

      has line form rules:

        has should prefix rule: # V7
          - should make every product acceptance line begin with the literal word 'should' immediately after "- "
          - should not add a separate '# should' marker on the parent 'has' line

        has read aloud rule: # V8
          - should sound natural when you speak the structural label then the rest of the '- should' line
          - should rename the owner or rewrite the requirement until that sentence sounds normal

      has requirement rules:

        has requirement wording rule: # V9
          - should state observable or verifiable outcomes after 'should'
          - should use vocabulary natural to the system being specified
          - should avoid unnecessary jargon in the meta rules file itself

        has behavioral grouping rule: # V11 V13
          - should split overload by nesting deeper (intermediate parents with two or three children), not by adding a fourth sibling
          - should move each '- should' line under the smallest structural owner that owns that behaviour
          - should make every '- should' line checkable in principle (test, inspection, measurement, or review)
          - should cover every agreed requirement from the source brief with no orphans

      has owner reference rule: # V15
        - should define every '*name' in a '- should' line with a matching '&name' on an earlier 'has' line
        - should name anchors consistently within one document
        - should put every word after '*anchor' on the same line into a # comment
        - should use this delegation shape: '- should call: *anchor # when or why'
        - should state each behavioural requirement under one structural owner
        - should let another owner delegate with '- should call: *anchor # …' instead of duplicating the same requirement

  has drafting process: # from brief to <system>.yjml

    has content drafting: # passes 1–3

      has requirement harvester: # pass 1
        - should list every required behaviour from the brief in plain language (no tree yet)
        - should include normal operation, failures, edges, exclusions, and example situations

      has behavioral drafter: # pass 2
        - should turn that list into '- should' lines (fix wording here; change later only for grammar)
        - should count those lines for the final coverage check

      has entity mapper: # pass 3
        - should list the real parts, assemblies, and roles that will own requirements
        - should add structural parents and children before attaching '- should' lines
        - should map each '- should' line to the smallest structural owner that truly owns that behaviour

    has shape drafting: # passes 4–6

      has tree assembler: # pass 4 — two-or-three-wide first cut
        - should set the single system root key with two or three top-level structural areas
        - should refine downward whole-to-part with two or three child 'has' owners per parent
        - should enforce composition ordering and nest heavy '- should' lists under new intermediate owners

      has nesting refiner: # pass 5
        - should fix any parent with four or more child 'has' owners by inserting intermediate assemblies
        - should allow exactly one four-wide parent only if deeper nesting would misrepresent the system
        - should otherwise leave the document with no four-wide parents
        - should walk each intermediate parent and apply the lineage-purpose rule; remove or re-home nodes that fail it

      has conformity checker: # pass 6
        - should confirm one root, only 'has' and '- should', two-space indent, labels, read-aloud, anchors, and sibling ordering
        - should confirm almost every parent has two or three child 'has' owners; flag unexplained four-wide parents
        - should confirm each structural parent has a clear purpose deducible from descendants alone, without leaning on the parent # comment
        - should confirm '- should' count matches the pass-1 harvest
